WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.750
INTRO:
In the world of big tech, we often hear customer

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.110
numbers in the billions.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:08.600
Last year, for example, Apple crossed 1 billion
active iPhone users.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.260
Similarly, Google also boasts 1 billion active
users.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:17.380
But, by far the biggest is Facebook who has
nearly 3 billion active users.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.420
Considering these mindboggling numbers and
the fact that these companies are basically

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.590
ubiquitous, it’s not surprising that these
companies are valued in the trillions.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.000
But, what does surprise me though are background
tech companies that the average person has

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.300
never heard of.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.449
Such companies generally serve corporate customers
and have little need to appeal to ordinary

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.559
people, yet they boast market caps in the
hundreds of billions.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:43.640
And one of the best examples of this is Salesforce.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.930
In 2017, Salesforce announced that they have
more than 150,000 customers.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.219
Even if we assume this number has grown to
200,000 customers, given that Salesforce is

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:57.760
valued at $228 billion, each customer is worth
well over a million dollars in terms of market

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.760
cap.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.269
To put that in perspective, each Facebook
customer is only worth $225 worth of market

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:08.119
cap, and even each Apple customer is only
worth $2800 worth of market cap.

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:12.549
So, here’s what Salesforce does and how
they became one of the most lucrative businesses

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:14.560
in the world.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.049
MARC BENIOFF:
Taking a look back, the story of Salesforce

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:25.659
dates back to a man named Marc Benioff who
was born on September 25, 1964, in San Francisco,

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:26.659
California.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.250
Marc’s father owned a local department store
in San Francisco which provided the family

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.920
with a pretty comfortable life.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.799
But, Marc’s father refused to spoil Marc.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.229
He felt that Marc should work for everything
he wanted.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:42.380
So, when Marc wanted a computer as a teenager,
his father told him to go get a job.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.521
Marc ended up becoming a janitor at a jewelry
store, but unfortunately, this didn’t last

00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:50.229
too long as Marc would be fired for using
the wrong soap on the floors.

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.299
Fortunately, though, he was able to save up
enough money to buy a computer.

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:58.509
Most kids nowadays want a computer to play
games, but Marc actually wanted a computer

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:00.159
to develop games.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:04.650
Marc started off small developing an application
called How to Juggle which he would sell for

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:05.650
$75.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.179
But once he saw this first paycheck, he would
go all-in on game development.

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:13.700
At 15, he founded a one-man company called
Liberty Software which developed games for

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.700
the Atari 8-bit.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.060
And over the next year, he published dozens
of games including Flapper, King Arthur’s

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.120
Heir, The Nightmare, Escape from Vulcan’s
Isle, and Crypt of the Undead.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.980
He worked with a company called Epyx to bring
his games to market, and by the time he was

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.420
16, he was earning $1,500 a month just from
royalties.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.400
Adjusting for inflation, that’s over $5000
per month today.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.489
So, Marc was basically making $60,000 per
year as a 16-year-old.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:42.120
It’s no wonder why this guy became a billionaire.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.760
Anyway, Marc ended up attending the University
of Southern California where he majored in

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.459
business administration.

00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:51.050
While completing his bachelor’s, Marc took
on internships as a programmer at Apple’s

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.250
Macintosh division.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.680
Marc enjoyed this experience and wanted to
continue his journey as a programmer, but

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.090
one of his university professors advised that
customer-oriented work would be much more

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.620
beneficial for Marc.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.150
So, Marc got a sales job at Oracle after graduation.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.420
While sales was not Marc’s area of expertise,
he shined here as well.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.000
Just one year after joining, Marc was named
Oracle’s Rookie of the Year at age 23.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.519
And just 3 years after that, Marc became the
youngest person in the company’s history

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:19.310
to become vice president.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.769
Considering the massive salaries of executives,
Marc was able to become a millionaire by the

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.510
time he was 25.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.069
Marc could’ve easily stayed at Oracle and
possibly even worked his way up to CEO, but

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:33.409
after 13 years at Oracle, he decided that
he wanted to do something bigger.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:36.670
Marc felt that all software companies were
approaching software incorrectly.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.470
They treated software as a product.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:43.910
Every few years companies like Adobe and Microsoft
came out with a new version of Windows or

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:44.939
Photoshop.

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:49.269
But Marc felt that it would be way better
if these companies treated software as a service

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:52.080
and constantly provided updates along the
way.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.250
Given that this is basically what all software
companies do today, Marc was clearly onto

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.890
something.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.349
And in February of 1999, he would start trying
to make this vision a reality by launching

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:03.390
SalesForce.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.220
SALESFORCE:
Marc didn’t try to start SalesForce by himself

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.220
though.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.120
He brought with him 3 Oracle veterans named
Parker Harris, Dave Moellenhoff, and Frank

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.120
Dominguez.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.840
Initially, Larry Ellison, Oracle’s founder,
was extremely supportive of Marc starting

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:17.930
his own business.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:22.510
In fact, he funded the startup with $2 million
and even became a board member.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.840
But, when he realized the SalesForce was also
trying to compete against Oracle, Larry stopped

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.720
helping SalesForce, but he did keep his stake.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.130
Now, that’s all cool and all, but what exactly
did SalesForce even do?

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.810
Well, the bread and butter of SalesForce was
and still is Customer Relationship Management

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:38.810
or CRM software.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:43.530
CRM software allows companies to aggregate
all of their sales, marketing, and customer

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.500
service data into one easy-to-use platform.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.680
This makes it much easier for companies to
analyze their demographics in extreme detail.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.750
For example, what’s your conversion rate,
how many unique customers do you have, how

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.340
many customers are repeat customers, how do
sales vary with the age and location of your

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.770
customers, and so on and so forth.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.430
Companies can then turn around and use this
data to address shortfalls, single out certain

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.680
demographics, and craft targeted marketing
campaigns that are super effective.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.370
So basically, CRM software takes care of the
logistics of managing customer data.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.400
Now, providing CRM solutions wasn’t a new
idea by any means.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.200
There were plenty of large companies already
providing CRM solutions like Oracle and Microsoft.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.130
But, as we previously touched on, these guys
were selling the software as a product.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:32.210
Customers had to buy the software, install
it onto their computers and servers, store

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.350
all the data themselves, and troubleshoot
many issues themselves.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:40.289
But, with SalesForce, SalesForce will store
the data, SalesForce will run the servers,

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:42.830
and SalesForce will run the software.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.660
Customers simply had to use a web portal to
access everything they need from anywhere

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.740
in the world.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:51.139
The convenience and accessibility the SalesForce
provided were unbeatable, but there were a

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:52.330
few concerns.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.160
First of all, companies had to trust SalesForce
with customer data, and secondly, this was

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.740
not a one-time purchase, it was a subscription.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.789
But, as more and more corporations switched
over to SalesForce, companies had an easier

00:06:03.789 --> 00:06:05.430
time trusting the SalesForce.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.740
But, it wasn’t all smooth sailing.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.319
Like all tech companies that were founded
before 2000, SalesForce had to navigate the

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:12.759
dot-com crash.

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:16.139
And this was particularly rough given that
SalesForce was an extremely young company

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:17.139
when the bubble burst.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.979
To ease the financial stress on the company,
Marc took SalesForce public in June of 2004

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:24.610
which allowed them to raise $110 million.

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.479
This helped them survive the dot-com crash,
but Marc knew that he had to diversify their

00:06:28.479 --> 00:06:32.009
income in order to better weather future recessions.

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:36.819
DELAYING PROFITABILITY:
One of Marc’s first diversification efforts

00:06:36.819 --> 00:06:40.080
came in 2005 when SalesForce introduced AppExchange.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.449
As the name suggests, AppExchange was basically
an app store but for businesses.

00:06:45.449 --> 00:06:48.759
SalesForce customers could download these
apps and SalesForce would split the revenue

00:06:48.759 --> 00:06:50.259
with the app developers.

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:54.919
Now, this probably doesn’t sound that crazy
today, but keep in mind that this was years

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:56.840
before the iPhone app store.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.849
Another diversification effort from SalesForce
came in 2009 with the launch of Service Cloud.

00:07:01.849 --> 00:07:05.810
Similar to AppExchange, Service Cloud was
another corporate application of an everyday

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:06.810
service.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:10.199
If you have a technical question, one of the
first things you’ll probably do is Google

00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:16.010
it, but it’s not so easy if you’re a company
and have a question about databases or servers.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:21.250
So, with Service Cloud, Marc basically launched
a Google/Quora type service for corporations.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:26.620
Similarly, in 2011, Marc launched Chatter
which was a social collaboration service for

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:27.770
companies.

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:31.570
All these diversification efforts did help
Salesforce’s topline as revenue exploded

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.789
from hundreds of millions to billions per
year.

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:38.340
But, Salesforce’s bottom line wasn’t looking
that great because Salesforce was reinvesting

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.139
all of their profits into their employees
and acquisitions.

00:07:41.139 --> 00:07:45.250
The acquisition part is self-explanatory,
but for people who aren’t familiar with

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.970
Salesforce, they probably aren’t aware of
Salesforce’s insane salaries.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:56.129
Software engineers, for example, earn anywhere
between $182,000 to $754,000 per year.

00:07:56.129 --> 00:08:00.870
Similarly, managers earn anywhere between
$248,000 to $1 million.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:06.090
At Salesforce, you can literally earn 7 figure
salary without being a C suite executive.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.270
Considering these salaries, I don’t think
you’d be surprised to hear that Salesforce

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.250
is the 9th highest paying company in the world.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:17.000
Aside from high salaries, Salesforce has one
of the best workplaces in the world with Salesforce

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.819
boasting an A+ in terms of workplace culture.

00:08:19.819 --> 00:08:22.190
Even Apple only scores a B+.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:26.270
This has earned Salesforce the title of 2nd
best place to work for in the world according

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.270
to Fortune.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:32.410
Marc also ranks as the 15th most approved
CEO in the US by employees, so clearly, Marc

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:36.590
knew what he was doing, and turning a profit
simply wasn’t a priority for him.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:40.310
He wanted to get Salesforce’s holdings,
diversification, and employees to a certain

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:43.330
level of satisfaction before focusing on profits.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.720
And in 2016, the time had finally come.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.200
Ever since then, Salesforce has not only been
pulling in billions every year but keeping

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.470
billions.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:57.630
SALESFORCE TODAY:
Today, Salesforce serves 90% of the Fortune

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:02.200
500 including everyone from Apple and Facebook
to Walmart and Mastercard.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:06.290
And given that each of these guys pays Salesforce
millions of dollars per year if not tens of

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:11.470
millions for their services, it’s not surprising
that Salesforce pulled in $25 billion in revenue

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.630
in the most recent 12 month period.

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.730
Aside from being a giant within the corporate
world, Salesforce practices what Marc calls

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.110
the 1-1-1 philanthropy model.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:25.160
Every year, Salesforce dedicates 1% of the
company’s equity, 1% of their products,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.580
and 1% of their employees’ time to philanthropic
efforts.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.830
So far, Salesforce has given their software
to 46,000 nonprofits and NGOs for free or

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:37.630
at a discount, and their employees have volunteered
a total of 4.9 million hours.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:43.440
As for Marc, he only owns a modest 3-4% of
the company, but given the size of Salesforce,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.160
that itself translates to $9 billion.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.420
Marc of course enjoys his wealth by splurging
on toys like mansions and cars, but Marc also

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.510
donates significant amounts of his wealth.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:56.060
He’s a very large proponent of taking action
against climate change.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:01.150
In fact, just a couple of months ago, he donated
$300 million to fight climate change.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:05.070
$200 million of this came from Marc himself
and $100 million came from Salesforce.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:09.120
And do you guys remember the teamtrees effort
from Mr. Beast a couple of years ago?

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:13.950
Mr. Beast was able to raise a total of $23
million which translates to 23 million trees

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:15.110
being planted.

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:19.720
There were some high-profile figures who donated
to this cause one of them being Marc who donated

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.100
$900,000.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.890
Not only did Marc donate to teemtrees, but
he went ahead and launched his own tree-planting

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.880
foundation called 1t.org.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.540
As the name suggests, the goal of the foundation
is to plant 1 trillion trees by 2050.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:37.630
And given the connections that Marc has, he
was able to get over 300 companies on board.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:42.640
So, hopefully, this vision does become a reality,
but we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.030
Do you guys think Marc can plant 1 trillion
trees?

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.350
Comment that down below.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.150
Also, drop a like if you hope Marc succeeds.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:53.290
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:59.770
and consider subscribing to see more questions
